  "register": "documentary, and careful to separate the network from the internet it became part of",
  "summary": "A Defense Department research program joined incompatible university and contractor computers through identical packet switches called IMPs, so a host needed only to speak to its own local switch rather than reproduce every other machine on the network. It carried remote logins, files, and above all electronic mail across leased telephone circuits administered from one contractor's office in Cambridge, Massachusetts, and it did not collapse so much as succeed itself out of existence: once its own internetworking protocol could run over other carriers, the original switches became one obsolete option among many, and the last of them was shut down in 1990.",
